WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.920
[Piano music]

00:00:01.294 --> 00:00:06.320
[Antani] I went to school for computer engineering&nbsp;
in India. I've worked with image processing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.840
computer vision, pattern recognition, machine&nbsp;
learning. So, my world was filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:18.880
developing algorithms that could extract&nbsp;
interesting objects from images and videos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:25.280
Pattern Recognition is a family of techniques&nbsp;
that looks for particular pixel characteristics or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:31.600
voxel characteristics inside an image and learns&nbsp;
to recognize those objects. Deep Learning is a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:37.840
of capturing the knowledge inside an image and&nbsp;
encapsulating it and then researchers like me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:45.600
spend time advancing newer deep learning networks&nbsp;
that look more broadly into an image, recognizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:52.000
these objects, recognizing organs in my case,&nbsp;
and diseases, and converting those visuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.480
into numerical risk predictors that could be used&nbsp;
by clinicians. So, my research is currently in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.400
three very different areas. One area looks at&nbsp;
Cervical Cancer. A machine could look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.720
images and be a very solid predictor of the risk&nbsp;
to the woman of developing Cervical pre-Cancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:16.000
encouraging early treatment. Another area I work&nbsp;
with, Sickle Cell Disease. One of the risk factors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.400
in Sickle Cell Disease is Cardiac Myopathy or&nbsp;
Cardiac Muscle Disease, which leads to stroke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.800
and perhaps even death. Looking at cardiac echo&nbsp;
videos and using AI to be a solid predictor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.640
along with other blood lab tests, improves&nbsp;
the chances of survival. A third area that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:39.520
I'm interested in is understanding the expression&nbsp;
of Tuberculosis in chest X-rays, particularly for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:45.360
children and those that are HIV-positive. The&nbsp;
expression of disease in that sub-population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:51.280
is very different from adults with TB, who are&nbsp;
not HIV positive. Every clinician have seen a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.640
certain number of patients in their clinical&nbsp;
training. They perhaps have spent more time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.840
at hospitals or clinical centers, and exposed to&nbsp;
certain population, and they become very adept at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:07.440
that population. Machines, on the other hand,&nbsp;
could be trained on data that is free of bias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.080
from different parts of the world, different&nbsp;
ethnicities, different age groups, so that there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.939
an improved care giving and therefore, a&nbsp;
better expectation on treatment and care.

00:02:17.536 --> 00:02:20.301
[Piano music]

